<section class="block-section story">
  <span class="section-tag">Story</span>
  <h2>What today is about</h2>
  <p class="sub">Same target as Block 2. Fewer moving parts. One path, in order.</p>
  <p>Block 2 asked you to do a lot at once: three readings, a handout, a sketch, a choice of three paths, an AI partner, and an exit ticket with four requirements. Some of you got there. Many of you did not, and that is on the design of the day, not on you. So today we do it again, one step at a time, and every step tells you what "done" looks like before you start it.</p>
  <p>The target has not changed. By the end of class you have a diagram with Earth's four spheres, three arrows with verbs, and one loop labeled + or − with a sentence saying why. That diagram is the thinking that every unit after this one builds on, which is why it is worth a second day.</p>
  <div class="callout support">
    <div class="callout-title">Not today</div>
    <p>No AI partner. No Field Notebook sketch. No Power outline. No readings to finish first. You need <strong>paper, a pencil, this page, and your phone for one photo at the end</strong>. If the Class Notebook header (Step 1) is a struggle, skip it and come back to it after you submit.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="callout good">
    <div class="callout-title">Already finished Block 2 with all four ticks?</div>
    <p>Start at <strong>Step 7</strong>. When you reach Step 8, pick a card from the <a href="/units/unit-1/systems-gallery/">Systems Gallery</a> and draw it. Then submit.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="block-section concepts">
  <span class="section-tag">Concepts</span>
  <h2>The only reference you need today</h2>
  <p class="sub">Keep this open. Everything below fits on a single screen, and it is also on the printed checklist.</p>
  {% include "partials/power-seeds.njk" %}
  <table style="margin-top: 12px;">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Sphere</th><th>What it is</th><th>Something you can see from AISC</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Atmosphere</strong></td><td>The air: gases around the planet</td><td>The monsoon clouds; the humidity you walk into at 3 pm</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Hydrosphere</strong></td><td>All the water: oceans, rivers, ice, groundwater, vapor</td><td>The Bay of Bengal; the water tanker; the Adyar river</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Geosphere</strong></td><td>The rock: crust, mantle, core, soil, sand</td><td>Marina Beach sand; the red laterite soil in the garden</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Biosphere</strong></td><td>All living things, and what they make</td><td>The crows, the neem trees, you</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div id="build" style="margin-top: 16px;"></div>
  {%- set carousel = { deck: systemsTutorialSlides.decks.build, id: "build", heading: "Watch the diagram grow", sub: "Nine frames, one per step. Open the frame for the step you are on." } %}
  {% include "partials/slide-carousel.njk" %}
  <div class="entry-grid" style="margin-top: 16px;">
    <div class="entry-card">
      <div class="entry-title">An arrow needs a verb</div>
      <div>An arrow between two spheres is only an arrow if it has a verb on it. "Ocean → atmosphere" says nothing. "Ocean <em>evaporates into</em> atmosphere" is a claim you can check. Verbs that work: evaporates, rains onto, weathers, dissolves, absorbs, releases, buries, erodes, breathes out.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="entry-card">
      <div class="entry-title">The sign test, in two lines</div>
      <div>Push one thing in the loop up. Follow the arrows round. If it comes back <strong>even more up</strong>, the loop is <strong>positive (+)</strong>. If it comes back <strong>pushed back down</strong>, the loop is <strong>negative (−)</strong>. Positive is not "good"; it means the change grows.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="entry-card">
      <div class="entry-title">Worked example: ice and sunlight</div>
      <div>Warmer air → less ice → darker ocean where the ice was → more sunlight absorbed → warmer air. Warm came back as <em>more</em> warm. That loop is <strong>+</strong>.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">The water, carbon, and oxygen cycles are the same drawing, extended</h3>
  <p class="rail-sub">Each cycle starts as one link between two spheres (your Step 3), then grows to touch all four (your Step 7). The last column is the loop you can sign in Step 6. The back of the printed checklist has a finished example.</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Cycle</th><th>The Step 3 link</th><th>How it grows to four spheres</th><th>A loop in it you can sign</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Water</strong></td><td>Hydrosphere <em>evaporates into</em> atmosphere; atmosphere <em>rains onto</em> hydrosphere</td><td>Rain <em>soaks into</em> soil and rock (geosphere); rivers <em>erode</em> rock into the sea; plants <em>breathe out</em> water vapor (biosphere)</td><td>Water-vapor loop: more evaporation → more vapor → more heat trapped → more evaporation. <strong>+</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Carbon</strong></td><td>Biosphere <em>takes CO₂ from</em> atmosphere (photosynthesis); biosphere <em>breathes CO₂ out into</em> atmosphere (respiration)</td><td>Ocean <em>absorbs</em> CO₂ (hydrosphere); rain <em>weathers</em> rock and <em>buries</em> carbon as limestone (geosphere); volcanoes <em>release</em> it back</td><td>Rock thermostat: warmer → more rain and weathering → more CO₂ buried → less heat trapped → cooler. Comes back smaller. <strong>−</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Oxygen</strong></td><td>Biosphere <em>releases O₂ into</em> atmosphere (plants); biosphere <em>breathes O₂ in from</em> atmosphere (animals)</td><td>O₂ <em>dissolves into</em> the sea for fish (hydrosphere); O₂ <em>rusts</em> iron in rock and soil (geosphere)</td><td>No easy sign: oxygen is a cycle, not a feedback loop. If you started with (c), sign the <strong>carbon</strong> loop instead: more plants → less CO₂ → cooler → plants grow slower. <strong>−</strong></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="callout warn" style="margin-top: 12px;">
    <div class="callout-title">Cycle or loop?</div>
    A <strong>cycle</strong> follows one kind of matter (water, carbon, oxygen) round the spheres and back. A <strong>feedback loop</strong> asks what happens to a push. Every cycle contains loops, but a cycle itself does not get a + or −. Your exit ticket needs one loop with a sign, not a whole cycle.
  </div>
  <p class="entry-meta" style="margin-top: 12px;">Want more than this? <a href="/units/unit-1/reading-four-spheres/">Four Spheres, One System</a> is the reading, but it is not required today.</p>
</section>

<section class="block-section do">
  <span class="section-tag">Do</span>
  <h2>Today's work: nine steps, in order</h2>
  <p class="sub">Tick each step when you meet its "done" line. Ticks save on this device so you can close the page and come back. Times add up to 80 minutes with slack; if you are behind at Step 5, tell me and we cut Step 7.</p>
  <div class="choice-intro">
    <strong>Learning Intention:</strong> {{ learningIntention | safe }}
  </div>

  <ol class="step-list" id="step-list">
    <li class="step">
      <label class="step-check-label"><input type="checkbox" class="step-check" data-step="0"><span class="step-head"><span class="step-num">Step 1</span> Notebook header <span class="step-time">2 min</span></span></label>
      <div class="step-body">
        <p>Open your Class Notebook and type today's header (it is below this list). Nothing else goes in the notebook until Step 9.</p>
        <p class="step-done"><strong>Done when:</strong> the header line is in your notebook. If this is the step that stalls you, skip it, and do it last.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li class="step">
      <label class="step-check-label"><input type="checkbox" class="step-check" data-step="1"><span class="step-head"><span class="step-num">Step 2</span> Four spheres, four rows <span class="step-time">12 min</span></span></label>
      <div class="step-body">
        <p>On paper, draw a table with four rows: atmosphere, hydrosphere, geosphere, biosphere. In each row write <strong>one thing that belongs to it that you have seen this week</strong>. Not from the table above: your own. Then turn to a partner and explain the sphere you wrote at the top of your page in the bell ringer. Thirty seconds each.</p>
        <p class="step-done"><strong>Done when:</strong> four rows, four examples, in your own words.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li class="step">
      <label class="step-check-label"><input type="checkbox" class="step-check" data-step="2"><span class="step-head"><span class="step-num">Step 3</span> One interaction, one sentence <span class="step-time">8 min</span></span></label>
      <div class="step-body">
        <p>Pick <strong>one</strong> pair from this list. Do not pick your own today; pick from here.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Hydrosphere → atmosphere: the sea <em>evaporates into</em> the air.</li>
          <li>Atmosphere → geosphere: rain <em>weathers</em> rock.</li>
          <li>Biosphere → atmosphere: plants <em>release oxygen into</em> the air.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Write it as one sentence on your paper in this shape: <strong>[sphere] [verb] [sphere]</strong>.</p>
        <p class="step-done"><strong>Done when:</strong> one sentence, with a verb, naming two spheres.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li class="step">
      <label class="step-check-label"><input type="checkbox" class="step-check" data-step="3"><span class="step-head"><span class="step-num">Step 4</span> Draw that sentence <span class="step-time">8 min</span></span></label>
      <div class="step-body">
        <p>Two circles, one arrow. Write the sphere names inside the circles and the verb on the arrow. That is a systems diagram. It is small, and it is correct.</p>
        <p class="step-done"><strong>Done when:</strong> two labeled circles, one arrow, one verb on the arrow.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li class="step">
      <label class="step-check-label"><input type="checkbox" class="step-check" data-step="4"><span class="step-head"><span class="step-num">Step 5</span> The return arrow <span class="step-time">10 min</span></span></label>
      <div class="step-body">
        <p>Something goes back the other way. Ask: <em>what does the second sphere send back to the first?</em> For the sea and the air, the air <em>rains onto</em> the sea. For rain and rock, the rock <em>releases dissolved minerals into</em> the water. For plants and air, the air <em>gives CO₂ to</em> the plants. Draw the second arrow, with its verb. You now have a loop.</p>
        <p class="step-done"><strong>Done when:</strong> two arrows between the same two circles, pointing opposite ways, each with a verb.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li class="step">
      <label class="step-check-label"><input type="checkbox" class="step-check" data-step="5"><span class="step-head"><span class="step-num">Step 6</span> Sign the loop <span class="step-time">10 min</span></span></label>
      <div class="step-body">
        <p>Run the sign test from the Concepts box. Push one thing up (more evaporation, more rain, more plants). Follow both arrows round once. Did the push come back bigger (+) or smaller (−)? Write <strong>+</strong> or <strong>−</strong> next to the loop, and under the diagram write one sentence starting with <em>"I pushed ___ up, and it came back ___ because ___."</em></p>
        <p class="step-done"><strong>Done when:</strong> a + or − on the loop, and the "I pushed" sentence underneath. Unsure of your sign? That is fine: write your best sign and say why. We check signs together at the end.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li class="step">
      <label class="step-check-label"><input type="checkbox" class="step-check" data-step="6"><span class="step-head"><span class="step-num">Step 7</span> Add the other two spheres <span class="step-time">12 min</span></span></label>
      <div class="step-body">
        <p>Draw the two spheres you have not used yet as two more circles. Connect each one to your diagram with <strong>one arrow and one verb</strong>. The cycles table in Concepts gives you the arrows for whichever starter you picked: the water cycle grows with <em>soaks into</em> and <em>breathe out</em>, the carbon cycle with <em>absorbs</em> and <em>buries</em>. Or use your Step 2 table for ideas: if you wrote "neem tree" under biosphere, what does a tree do to the soil (geosphere)? Its roots <em>break up</em> rock. That is your arrow.</p>
        <p class="step-done"><strong>Done when:</strong> four circles, at least three arrows in total, every arrow has a verb.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li class="step">
      <label class="step-check-label"><input type="checkbox" class="step-check" data-step="7"><span class="step-head"><span class="step-num">Step 8</span> Check against the exit ticket, then submit <span class="step-time">8 min</span></span></label>
      <div class="step-body">
        <p>Before you take the photo, tick these four on your paper:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>All four spheres labeled</li>
          <li>At least three arrows, each with a verb</li>
          <li>One loop marked + or −</li>
          <li>The "I pushed" sentence under the diagram</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Take one photo. Submit it in Google Classroom using the button in the Show section below. If your Block 2 diagram exists, put it beside today's in the same photo.</p>
        <p class="step-done"><strong>Done when:</strong> Classroom says "Turned in."</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li class="step">
      <label class="step-check-label"><input type="checkbox" class="step-check" data-step="8"><span class="step-head"><span class="step-num">Step 9</span> Reflect <span class="step-time">5 min</span></span></label>
      <div class="step-body">
        <p>Open the Reflection Journal from the Reflect section at the bottom. One question, a few sentences. Then, if you skipped Step 1, do the notebook header now.</p>
        <p class="step-done"><strong>Done when:</strong> the journal entry is saved.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p class="step-progress" id="step-progress" aria-live="polite"></p>
  <button type="button" class="step-reset" id="step-reset">Clear my ticks</button>

  {% include "partials/notebook-cue.njk" %}
</section>

